{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What makes a set different from a list?",
      "options": [
        "Each value is in it only once: duplicates are dropped",
        "It keeps its values sorted",
        "Its values are read by key",
        "It can't change after it's made"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "guests = {\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\", \"Tommy\"}\nprint(len(guests))",
      "options": ["4", "3", "1", "It stops with an error: Tommy is there twice"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "Which of these makes an empty set?",
      "options": ["{}", "[]", "set()", "()"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "What does `x = {}` make?",
      "options": [
        "An empty set",
        "An empty dictionary",
        "An empty list",
        "Nothing: it stops with an error"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "Which line adds \"shotgun\" to the set weapons?",
      "options": [
        "weapons.append(\"shotgun\")",
        "weapons[\"shotgun\"] = 1",
        "weapons + \"shotgun\"",
        "weapons.add(\"shotgun\")"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "Why does a set use `.add()` and not `.append()`, as a list does?",
      "options": [
        "A set has no order, so adding at the end means nothing",
        "append() works only on numbers",
        "add() keeps the values sorted",
        "A set can take only one new value"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "Which line takes \"bat\" out of the set weapons?",
      "options": [
        "del weapons[0]",
        "weapons[\"bat\"] = 0",
        "weapons.remove(\"bat\")",
        "weapons = weapons - \"bat\""
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "banned = {\"Sonny\", \"Diaz\", \"Gonzalez\"}\nplayer = \"Diaz\"\nif player in banned:\n    print(f\"{player} is banned!\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"{player} is welcome\")",
      "options": ["Diaz is banned!", "Diaz is welcome", "Tommy is welcome", "True"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "When is a set a better choice than a list?",
      "options": [
        "When the order of the values matters",
        "When each value must be there only once, or we often check whether a value is in it",
        "When we read the values by their position",
        "When we store key: value pairs"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "What do `{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}` and `{1, 2}` make?",
      "options": [
        "Two sets: both use curly braces",
        "Two dictionaries: both use curly braces",
        "A dictionary, then a set: the colons make the dictionary",
        "A set, then a dictionary: numbers make a dictionary"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "colors = {\"red\", \"blue\", \"red\", \"red\"}\nprint(len(colors))",
      "options": ["4", "2", "3", "1"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Tommy\", \"Cortez\", \"Lance\"]\nunique = list(set(names))\nprint(len(unique))",
      "options": ["5", "2", "3", "1"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "In `list(set(names))`, what is the set in the middle for?",
      "options": [
        "It drops the duplicates, then list() turns it back into a list",
        "It sorts the names, then list() keeps them in that order",
        "list() can't take a list, so it needs a set first",
        "It makes a copy of names that can't change"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "After `unique_names = list(set(names))`, are the names surely in the order they had in names?",
      "options": [
        "Yes: a set keeps the order they were added in",
        "No: a set doesn't keep order, so they may come out in another order",
        "No: they always come out sorted from A to Z",
        "Yes, but only when the names are text"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "seen = set()\nseen.add(\"A1\")\nseen.add(\"B2\")\nseen.add(\"A1\")\nprint(len(seen))",
      "options": ["3", "1", "0", "2"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "In the Signal Log mission, how many signals does `signals` hold, and how many different codes?",
      "options": [
        "8 signals, 4 different codes",
        "4 signals, 8 different codes",
        "8 signals, 8 different codes",
        "4 signals, 4 different codes"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "In the Signal Log mission, `\"D4\" in signals` would also find D4. Why check in the set?",
      "options": [
        "in doesn't work on a list",
        "The list would say D4 missing, since D4 is near the end",
        "The set keeps D4 first, so it is found sooner",
        "Both give the same answer, but checking in a set is much faster"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q18",
      "text": "In the Signal Log mission, what does the Unique line print if we write `len(signals)` instead of `len(unique)`?",
      "options": ["Unique: 4", "Unique: 1", "It stops with an error", "Unique: 8"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q19",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "codes = {\"A1\", \"B2\", \"C3\"}\nprint(\"D4\" in codes)\nprint(\"B2\" in codes)",
      "options": ["False\nTrue", "True\nFalse", "False\nFalse", "D4\nB2"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q20",
      "text": "Which expression is True when Phil is in the set crew?",
      "options": ["crew[\"Phil\"]", "crew == \"Phil\"", "crew.add(\"Phil\")", "\"Phil\" in crew"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q21",
      "text": "What does `.add()` do with a value that is already in the set?",
      "options": [
        "It stops with an error",
        "Nothing: the set stays as it was",
        "It adds a second copy",
        "It takes the value out"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q22",
      "text": "Which of these is a set?",
      "options": ["[\"a\", \"b\"]", "(\"a\", \"b\")", "{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}", "{\"a\", \"b\"}"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q23",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "nums = [3, 1, 3, 2, 1, 3]\nprint(len(nums))\nprint(len(set(nums)))",
      "options": ["3\n6", "6\n6", "6\n3", "3\n3"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q24",
      "text": "We write `guests = {\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\"}` and print it. What may come out different from how we wrote it?",
      "options": [
        "The order of the names",
        "How many names it shows",
        "The names' letters, which come out in capitals",
        "Nothing: a set prints exactly as written"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q25",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "weapons = {\"bat\", \"pistol\"}\nweapons.add(\"shotgun\")\nweapons.add(\"bat\")\nweapons.remove(\"pistol\")\nprint(len(weapons))",
      "options": ["3", "4", "2", "1"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q26",
      "text": "Which expression gives a set of the different values in the list scores?",
      "options": ["list(scores)", "set(scores)", "[scores]", "scores.set()"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q27",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "visited = set()\nfor planet in [\"Mars\", \"Venus\", \"Mars\", \"Earth\", \"Venus\"]:\n    visited.add(planet)\nprint(len(visited))",
      "options": ["5", "2", "3", "1"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q28",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "visited = set()\nfor planet in [\"Mars\", \"Venus\", \"Mars\"]:\n    if planet in visited:\n        print(f\"{planet} again\")\n    else:\n        visited.add(planet)\n        print(f\"{planet} new\")",
      "options": [
        "Mars new\nVenus new\nMars new",
        "Mars again\nVenus again\nMars again",
        "Mars new\nVenus new",
        "Mars new\nVenus new\nMars again"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q29",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "codes = {}\ncodes.add(\"A1\")",
      "options": [
        "It stops with an error: {} made a dictionary, and a dictionary has no add()",
        "codes holds A1: {} is an empty set",
        "A1 becomes a key of codes, with no value",
        "Nothing: add() waits for a second value"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q30",
      "text": "In the Signal Log mission, A1 is three times in signals. How many times is it in the set unique?",
      "options": [
        "Three times: the set copies the list",
        "Once: a set keeps each value only once",
        "Twice: only the first copy is dropped",
        "Not at all: a value that repeats is dropped"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    }
